[{"text":"DiracOrder[exp] orders the Dirac matrices in exp lexicographically. DiracOrder[exp, orderlist] orders the Dirac matrices in exp according to orderlist. DiracOrder is also an option of DiracSimplify and some other functions dealing with Dirac algebra. If set to True, the function DiracOrder will be applied to the intermediate result to reorder the Dirac matrices lexicographically.","anchor":"diracorder"},{"text":"Overview, DiracSimplify, DiracTrick.","anchor":"see-also","section":"See also"},{"text":"GA[\\[Beta], \\[Alpha]] DiracOrder[%]","anchor":"cb1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\beta }.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\alpha }\\]","anchor":"cb1-3","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[2 \\bar{g}^{\\alpha \\beta }-\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\alpha }.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\beta }\\]","anchor":"cb1-3","section":"Examples"},{"text":"DiracOrder also works with Dirac matrices in D-dimensions.","anchor":"cb1-3","section":"Examples"},{"text":"GAD[\\[Rho], \\[Nu], \\[Mu], \\[Nu]] DiracOrder[%]","anchor":"cb2","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\gamma ^{\\rho }.\\gamma ^{\\nu }.\\gamma ^{\\mu }.\\gamma ^{\\nu }\\]","anchor":"cb2-3","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[(D-2) \\gamma ^{\\mu }.\\gamma ^{\\rho }+2 (2-D) g^{\\mu \\rho }\\]","anchor":"cb2-3","section":"Examples"},{"text":"By default \\(\\gamma^5\\) is moved to the right.","anchor":"cb2-3","section":"Examples"},{"text":"GA[5, \\[Mu], \\[Nu]] DiracOrder[%]","anchor":"cb3","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\bar{\\gamma }^5.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\mu }.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\nu }\\]","anchor":"cb3-3","section":"Examples"},{"text":"GA[6, \\[Mu], 7] DiracOrder[%]","anchor":"cb4","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\bar{\\gamma }^6.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\mu }.\\bar{\\gamma }^7\\]","anchor":"cb4-3","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\mu }.\\bar{\\gamma }^7\\]","anchor":"cb4-3","section":"Examples"},{"text":"orderlist comes into play when we need an ordering that is not lexicographic","anchor":"cb4-3","section":"Examples"},{"text":"GA[\\[Alpha], \\[Beta], \\[Delta]] DiracOrder[%]","anchor":"cb5","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\alpha }.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\beta }.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\delta }\\]","anchor":"cb5-3","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\alpha }.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\beta }.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\delta }\\]","anchor":"cb5-3","section":"Examples"},{"text":"DiracOrder[GA[\\[Alpha], \\[Beta], \\[Delta]], {\\[Delta], \\[Beta], \\[Alpha]}]","anchor":"cb6","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[-\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\delta }.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\beta }.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\alpha }+2 \\bar{\\gamma }^{\\delta } \\bar{g}^{\\alpha \\beta }-2 \\bar{\\gamma }^{\\beta } \\bar{g}^{\\alpha \\delta }+2 \\bar{\\gamma }^{\\alpha } \\bar{g}^{\\beta \\delta }\\]","anchor":"cb6-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"Reordering of Dirac matrices in long chains is expensive, so that DiracSimplify does not do it by default.","anchor":"cb6-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"DiracSimplify[GAD[\\[Mu], \\[Nu]] + GAD[\\[Nu], \\[Mu]]]","anchor":"cb7","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\gamma ^{\\mu }.\\gamma ^{\\nu }+\\gamma ^{\\nu }.\\gamma ^{\\mu }\\]","anchor":"cb7-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"However, if you know that it can lead to simpler expressions, you can activate the reordering via the option DiracOrder.","anchor":"cb7-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"DiracSimplify[GAD[\\[Mu], \\[Nu]] + GAD[\\[Nu], \\[Mu]], DiracOrder -> True]","anchor":"cb8","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[2 g^{\\mu \\nu }\\]","anchor":"cb8-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"Reproduce Eq. 18.128 from An Introduction to Quantum Field Theory by M. Peskin and D. Schroeder.","anchor":"cb8-1","section":"Examples"},{"text":"DiracSimplify[1/2 (GAD[\\[Mu], \\[Alpha], \\[Nu]] + GAD[\\[Nu], \\[Alpha], \\[Mu]]), DiracOrder -> True]","anchor":"cb9","section":"Examples"},{"text":"\\[\\gamma ^{\\nu } g^{\\alpha \\mu }+\\gamma ^{\\mu } g^{\\alpha \\nu }-\\gamma ^{\\alpha } g^{\\mu \\nu }\\]","anchor":"cb9-1","section":"Examples"}]